--- a/docs/02_Architecture_and_Components.md
+++ b/docs/02_Architecture_and_Components.md
@@ -611,6 +611,33 @@ leave Grok, the editor clears unused full access; API validation rejects a full
 declared Grok primary or fallback route. Retained unused or disallowed full settings stay visible
 until cleared, so no hidden permission can return after a later route change. Changing the setting
 affects new work; it does not approve an existing offer.
+A standalone local xPerfect has no Core to inject that authority. There the local owner may mint a grant
+for one existing open host mission Worker of the `grok-build` profile with
+`POST /v1/workers/{id}/worker-access-grant {expires_at, deny_rules, proof}`. The proof is an HMAC over that
+Worker's ID, the expiry and the deny list, made with `data/worker-access-grant.key`: an owner-only file in this
+instance's private state, never an environment variable, `config.json` value or bearer token. The Worker's own
+row must be the instance owner's (`GLASSHIVE_DEFAULT_OWNER_ID`) in the `local` tenant; request headers never decide
+it. A delegation's Worker (a manager's helper or Core's work) never holds one, and neither does a Worker with Core
+or broker authority. The deny list must equal the instance's non-empty `WPR_GROK_DENY`. The entire grant is refused
+when Grok cannot express any rule. Supported Claude MCP rules use Grok's native `MCPTool` form. The expiry is at most
+a day away. The grant is an insert-only row, never a
+bundle field: create, assign, find-or-resume and delegation bundles that offer one are refused, and the exact same
+mint again returns the same row. Mint reads the stored restriction in the same write transaction as the insert.
+A restriction (`provider_capabilities.native_tools: false`) seen at mint, in any
+later stored bundle or in a run's own bundle is latched in the row for good and forced on every later run. At each
+run's admission the grant becomes the same run-local admitted generation and launch authority the Core path
+produces, bound to that Worker, run and host startup lease, with the grant's expiry as its deadline; a run
+admitted after expiry fails before launch as `worker_access_expired`. For that run only, the grant's deny rules
+are added to `[permission].deny` in the Worker's private Grok `config.toml` (with one keeping that file unedited;
+`grok agent` takes no `--deny` flag). The runner checks the manifest and the private config before Grok starts and
+again when the session opens. The run always starts a fresh process. Each question can accept a reply for up to
+60 seconds from publication, capped by the grant's expiry. At the grant's expiry the runner records
+`native_authority_expired` and kills its own supervised process group.
+With deny rules set, Grok's always-approve still asks about a shell command it cannot split into simple segments;
+under a grant such a request is refused at once with Grok's own reject option and never published, so no one,
+the Worker included (its home holds the mailbox), can approve it.
+An ungranted run or a coordinator keeps its config as it is. The native Grok gate is unchanged, so
+restricted/zero-tool mode still wins.

 The host owns internal lifecycle bookkeeping. Its work log and native journal preserve run
 progress and continuity without requiring the model to edit an internal log or obtain owner
